 ```
 
+### Incident Associations
+
+`incidents create` and `incidents update` can link an incident to other Rootly records. Each flag takes comma-separated slugs or IDs, like `rootly pulse create --services`:
+
+| Flag | Links | API attribute |
+|------|-------|---------------|
+| `--services` | Services | `service_ids` |
+| `--types` | Incident types | `incident_type_ids` |
+| `--functionalities` | Functionalities | `functionality_ids` |
+| `--environments` | Environments | `environment_ids` |
+| `--teams` | Teams | `group_ids` |
+| `--causes` | Causes | `cause_ids` |
+
+On `update`, only the flags you pass are sent. Each one replaces the incident's current list, and an empty value (`--services=`) clears it. `update` accepts a sequential ID such as `INC-123` as well as a UUID or slug.
+
+```bash
+rootly incidents create --title="Checkout errors" --services=api-gateway,payments --types=customer-impacting
+rootly incidents update INC-123 --services=api-gateway --teams=platform
+```
+
 ### Status-Page Templates and Maintenance Statuses
 
 List a status page's templates with `rootly status-pages templates list --status-page=<id>`. The command supports `--page`, `--page-size`, and the standard `--format` output flag.
 
 `status-pages events create` and `status-pages events update` accept incident statuses `investigating`, `identified`, `monitoring`, and `resolved`, plus scheduled-maintenance statuses `scheduled`, `in_progress`, and `completed`. For `status-pages events resolve`, `--status` defaults to `resolved`; use `--status=completed` to complete scheduled maintenance.
 
+Resolving an incident does not resolve its status-page events. After `incidents update`, `incidents create` or `alerts resolve --resolve-incidents` leaves an incident resolved, the CLI warns on stderr about any events still open and prints the `rootly status-pages events resolve <event-id>` command to run. The check is best-effort and never changes the command's output or exit code.
+
 ### Alert Escalation
 
 `rootly alerts escalate <alert-id>` escalates an alert using its current policy. Optionally pass `--escalation-policy=<uuid>` and/or `--level=<n>` to select a policy or escalation level; levels must be at least 1. The API's error title is shown when escalation cannot proceed.
